# Cloudflare Workers Static Assets default every response to
# "Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate" — safe, but it means a
# revalidation round-trip to the edge on every single visit, even though most of
# this rarely changes between deploys. Override with something that actually
# lets the browser skip that round-trip.
#
# /plugins/* is fetched with a "?v=APP_VERSION" cache-busting query string
# (see index.html's plugin loader), so a given URL's content never changes —
# safe to cache hard and long.
/plugins/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Everything else (index.html, favicon.svg) isn't versioned in its URL. This is
# the new-tab page — opened many times an hour — so "must-revalidate" was the
# wrong tradeoff: every single open, even seconds after the last one, forced a
# round-trip to the edge before the browser could paint anything, and on a slow
# or congested connection that round-trip is exactly the "few seconds instead of
# instant" a new tab should never have to pay. stale-while-revalidate fixes
# this: within max-age it's an instant same-cache hit with zero network
# involvement; past that (and within the swr window) the browser paints the
# cached copy immediately and revalidates in the background, so the NEXT tab
# gets anything new — nobody ever blocks on the network just to open a tab.
# Listed explicitly (not "/*") so this block never overlaps /plugins/* above —
# Cloudflare concatenates headers from every matching block rather than letting
# the more specific one win, so an overlapping catch-all corrupts both.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=86400
/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=86400
/favicon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=86400
